## 9. PHASE 6 — SECURE OTP AND SESSION MANAGEMENT

Implement security controls suitable for a public service.

### OTP security

- Cryptographically secure OTP generation.
- Short expiry time.
- Single-use challenges.
- Maximum verification attempts.
- Resend cooldown.
- Per-mobile rate limiting.
- Per-IP or equivalent abuse controls.
- Protection against OTP guessing.
- Protection against OTP replay.
- Protection against SMS-spam abuse.
- Invalidation of superseded challenges where appropriate.
- Generic authentication errors.
- Safe cleanup of expired challenges.
- No plaintext OTP logging.
- No OTP returned to production clients except through the configured delivery channel.

Choose concrete values based on the existing application's needs, provider restrictions, and security requirements. Document the selected values.

### Session security

Use an authentication mechanism appropriate to the existing application.

Where cookie-based sessions are appropriate:

- Use Secure cookies over HTTPS.
- Use HttpOnly.
- Use an appropriate SameSite policy.
- Rotate the session after successful authentication.
- Protect against CSRF where relevant.
- Set appropriate session expiration.
- Invalidate sessions on logout.
- Avoid putting authentication tokens in URLs.
- Enforce authorization on the backend for every protected route.

If the application uses a different established authentication architecture, adapt these controls appropriately.

### SMS provider

Inspect the existing SMS gateway integration.

Use the configured provider's secure API or documented integration.

- Keep API keys in environment variables or the provider's secret manager.
- Never expose provider credentials in frontend code.
- Do not hardcode real credentials.
- Do not use a development SMS console in production.
- Do not report OTP delivery success if the provider request failed.
- Provide a safe local test mode with synthetic OTPs.
- Ensure test-mode behavior cannot accidentally be enabled in production.

